+## Implemented and deferred components
+
+| Component | V1 status | May do | Must not do |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Rules/context parser | Implemented | Normalize explicit dates, relative dates and UI-selected entities | Expand regexes into an unrestricted natural-language interpreter |
+| Structured semantic parser | Implemented with configured provider fallback | Return a schema-validated multi-operation `SemanticIntentDraft` | Authorize execution, invent IDs, or write data |
+| Entity grounding | Implemented | Bind days, activities and existing place candidates to trip-owned IDs | Guess between duplicate or missing entities |
+| Policy/risk gate | Implemented | Route to answer, clarification, preview, or rejection and require approval for changes | Treat model output as user authorization |
+| Research Agent | Deferred | Future: query configured providers and persist verified candidates and sources | It is not a node in the current graph and must not be implied to have run |
+| Draft provider / Planner | Implemented | Select supplied place IDs and dates into a strict draft | Query the database, invent places, costs, sources, routes, or approval |
+| Route enrichment | Implemented | Resolve every adjacent route through the configured map adapter | Hide provider failures or silently fabricate production routes |
+| Deterministic validator | Implemented | Enforce ownership, dates, time, route, budget, source, protected-activity, and version rules | Delegate hard-constraint decisions to a model |
+| Reviewer correction | Implemented as one bounded re-draft | Feed validation errors and the previous plan back to the draft provider once | Override validation, loop without a bound, or approve a revision |
+| Orchestrator | Implemented | Route, checkpoint, interrupt, resume, and record outcomes | Make unconstrained product decisions |
+| Acceptance service | Implemented | Revalidate and atomically apply a current approved revision | Accept stale, invalid, rejected, or superseded revisions |
+
+The `researching` run status currently covers planning-context assembly and
+intent resolution. It does not mean that a Research Agent or live Web Search ran.
+
+## Authoritative contracts
+
+The Pydantic definitions in `backend/app/schemas.py` are authoritative:
+
+- `PlanningIntentParseRequest`: normalized non-blank message plus optional UI
+  context.
+- `SemanticIntentDraft`, `GroundedIntent`, and `IntentPolicyDecision`: strict
+  semantic, entity-binding, and workflow-policy layers carried by
+  `PlanningIntentResolution`.
+- `PlanningIntent` and `PlanningRequest`: backward-compatible planning fields
+  consumed by the current Planner prompt.
+- `PlanningRunCreate`: `initial_plan` or `replan` plus a required, non-blank
+  request of at most 4,000 characters.
+- Formal `Activity` responses carry the user-authored `importance` decision
+  semantic (`low`, `normal`, `high`, or `must_do`). It is supplied to the
+  Planner as a soft preference; deterministic sacrifice constraints are added
+  only with the corresponding Phase 2 validator slice.
+- `CandidatePlanSnapshot`: at most 100 unique candidate activities and at most
+  200 uniquely positioned route legs.
+- `PlanningClarificationInterrupt`: `kind`, one or more questions, and `run_id`.
+- `PlanningApprovalInterrupt`: `kind`, `run_id`, and `revision_id`.
+- `PlanningRunDecision`: only `clarify`, `accept`, or `reject`; clarification
+  requires a non-blank answer.
+- `PlanValidationResult`: hard errors and non-blocking warnings.
+
+Unknown fields are forbidden on intent and interrupt inputs. The semantic parser
+emits no operation mode or database identifiers. Its output must pass
+deterministic grounding and the policy gate before the graph may plan. The legacy
+four-value `PlanningIntent` vocabulary remains only as a compatibility field;
+workflow routing uses the structured operation list and policy decision.
+
+## Persisted records and ownership
+
+| Record | Owner | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `Trip`, `DayPlan`, `Activity`, `RouteLeg` | Business services | Formal user-visible state |
+| `PlaceCandidate`, `SourceRecord` | Research/import services | Trip-owned facts available to planning |
+| `PlanningRun` | Orchestrator/repository | Lifecycle, base trip version, provider metadata, usage, failure, and pending interrupt |
+| `PlanRevision` | Planning repository | Immutable before/proposed snapshots, validation result, sources, and decision |
+| `CandidateRouteLeg` | Route enrichment/repository | Auditable route evidence tied to a revision |
+| LangGraph checkpoints | LangGraph runtime | Recoverable execution state, keyed by `PlanningRun.id` as `thread_id` |
+
+`PlanningRun`, `PlanRevision`, and candidate evidence are the product and audit
+source of truth. LangGraph checkpoints are execution infrastructure and must not
+replace product records.
+
+## Graph state and serialization boundary
+
+`PlanningGraphState` may contain only JSON-serializable values:
+
+| Field | Producer | Consumer |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `run_id`, `trip_id`, `user_request` | Runtime | All nodes |
+| `entry_point`, `selected_day_id`, `selected_activity_id` | Persisted `PlanningRun` UI context | Intent resolver and grounding |
+| `planning_context` | `resolve_intent` | Planner and revision persistence; includes structured goals, persisted formal route facts and selected `decision_context` |
+| `intent_resolution` | `resolve_intent` | Router and Planner |
+| `planning_goals` | `resolve_intent` | Planner, Goal Satisfaction Check, and revision persistence |
+| `clarification_count` | Runtime / `clarify` | Two-round clarification bound |
+| `proposed_snapshot` | `plan`, then `validate` after route enrichment | Validator and persistence |
+| `validation_result` | `validate` | Conditional router and correction prompt |
+| `review_count` | `review` | Single-correction bound |
+| `goal_satisfaction` | `goal_check` | Goal repair router and revision persistence |
+| `goal_review_count`, `goal_feedback` | `goal_repair` | One bounded repair for unmet hard goals |
+| `revision_id` | `persist` | Approval and acceptance |
+| `outcome` | `accept` or `reject` | Terminal result |
+
+Database sessions, ORM entities, API clients, secrets, and executable objects are
+forbidden in graph state. The public run-creation API persists UI selection
+context and rejects selected day/activity IDs that do not belong to the trip.
+
+## State machine
+
+```text
+queued
+  -> researching
+  -> awaiting_clarification -> researching       (at most two answers)
+  -> planning
+  -> validating
+       -> planning                               (one correction only)
+       -> awaiting_confirmation
+  -> goal_check
+       -> planning                               (at most one hard-goal repair)
+       -> awaiting_confirmation
+  -> accepted | rejected | failed
+```
+
+The implemented graph nodes are:
+
+```text
+START -> resolve_intent -> clarify? -> plan -> validate
+      -> review? -> plan -> validate -> goal_check
+      -> goal_repair? -> plan -> validate -> goal_check -> persist -> approval
+      -> accept | reject -> END
+```
+
+`cancelled` is a reserved persisted status; Agent v1 exposes no cancellation
+transition. A second validation failure terminates the run with
+`planner_constraints_unsatisfied` and creates no pending revision. A valid
+candidate that still misses an explicit deterministic hard Planning Goal is
+given one structured repair attempt; a second miss terminates with
+`goal_constraints_unsatisfied`. Soft goals are observations only and never
+block a revision.
+
+Human clarification and final approval use explicit interrupts. Side effects
+before an interrupt must be idempotent. Resuming with the wrong decision kind or
+resuming a terminal run is rejected.
+
+## Candidate evidence and hard validation
+
+Every candidate activity must reference a trip-owned `PlaceCandidate` and its
+trip-owned `SourceRecord`. Costs are copied from backend records, never trusted
+from the model. Every adjacent pair in the complete candidate day, including
+formal locked, active, or completed activities that remain in place, must have a
+`CandidateRouteLeg`.
+
+Acceptance re-runs validation while locking the revision, run, and trip. At
+minimum, these conditions block acceptance:
+
+- unknown or cross-trip place/source/replacement IDs;
+- date outside the trip or daily time window;
+- overlapping activities or insufficient route time;
+- changed locked, active, or completed activities;
+- omitted or changed activities outside the grounded day/activity scope;
+- new activities outside a grounded target day;
+- increased estimated cost when the grounded constraint forbids an increase;
+- daily or total budget violation when a Trip budget limit is configured;
+- missing or structurally inconsistent route evidence;
+- formal trip version different from `base_trip_version`;
+- revision or run no longer pending confirmation.
+
+Rejecting a candidate changes only the run and revision decision records. It
+must not change formal activities, routes, or the trip version.
+
+## Provider and tool policy
+
+### Draft provider
+
+- Production configuration is injected through `Settings`; secrets never enter
+  graph state, prompts, responses, or database snapshots.
+- The Responses API is called with JSON Schema output and `store=false`.
+- Provider timeout and SDK retry count are bounded by
+  `PLANNER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` and `PLANNER_MAX_RETRIES`. The outer planning
+  execution also has an `AGENT_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` wall-clock deadline; an
+  expiry marks the run failed as `planning_run_timeout` and releases its worker.
+- Agent workers claim up to `AGENT_WORKER_CONCURRENCY` independent runs at once
+  (default 2), so one slow provider call cannot block the whole queue.
+- The model may choose only supplied place IDs and trip dates. Backend code
+  resolves costs, sources, replacement IDs, and routes.
+- Candidate names, descriptions, tags, and user text are untrusted data, not
+  instructions.
+
+### Semantic intent provider
+
+- High-confidence dates and UI references are resolved by deterministic code.
+- Requests left as legacy `unknown` use the configured DeepSeek/OpenAI-compatible
+  Responses endpoint with the `SemanticIntentDraft` JSON Schema.
+- The provider extracts semantic operations and mentions only. Backend grounding
+  owns IDs, completeness, risk, confirmation, and workflow routing.
+
+### Map provider
+
+- Production requires `AMAP_REST_KEY`.
+- `ALLOW_MAP_ESTIMATES=true` is permitted only for tests and explicit local
+  development. It is not a silent production fallback.
+- Provider rejection, credentials failure, quota exhaustion, network failure,
+  and missing configuration remain visible as stable failures.
+
+### Deferred tools
+
+Weather, Web Search, OCR/multimodal import, and community-content extraction are
+outside Agent v1. They require separate input/output, source, timeout, retry,
+cache, privacy, and failure contracts before becoming graph nodes.
+
+## Stable planning failure codes
+
+| Code | Meaning | Retry policy |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `planner_not_configured` | Key or model missing | Configuration change required |
+| `planner_authentication_failed` | Credentials rejected | Do not retry unchanged credentials |
+| `planner_permission_denied` | Model/account permission denied | Do not retry unchanged configuration |
+| `planner_rate_limited` | Provider quota or rate limit | SDK may retry within configured bound |
+| `planner_timeout` | Provider timeout | SDK may retry within configured bound |
+| `planner_unavailable` | Connection failure | SDK may retry within configured bound |
+| `planner_incomplete_output` | Incomplete or filtered output | Fail the run |
+| `planner_invalid_output` | Schema or ownership violation | Fail the run; do not coerce |
+| `planner_provider_error` | Provider 5xx response | SDK may retry within configured bound |
+| `planner_request_rejected` | Provider 4xx response | Do not retry unchanged request |
+| `planner_constraints_unsatisfied` | Second deterministic validation failed | Fail with validation codes; no revision |
+| `goal_constraints_unsatisfied` | A valid candidate still misses a hard goal after one repair | Fail with structured goal evidence; no revision |
+| `decision_context_invalid` | Planner precondition has contradictory or dangling facts | Fail before model invocation with invariant codes |
+| `planning_run_timeout` | The end-to-end worker deadline expired | Fail visibly and release the worker; retry as a new run after resolving provider latency |
+| `planning_graph_failed` | Unexpected runtime failure | Preserve sanitized diagnostic text and investigate |
+
+Failure messages returned or logged must not contain API keys, authorization
+headers, raw private trip payloads, or full provider responses.
+
+## API boundary
+
+Agent v1 uses these endpoints:
+
+| Method and path | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GET /api/v1/trips/{trip_id}/planning-context` | Read the normalized, read-only planning input |
+| `POST /api/v1/trips/{trip_id}/planning-intent/resolve` | Preview strict intent resolution |
+| `POST /api/v1/trips/{trip_id}/planning-runs` | Queue a non-blank planning request |
+| `GET /api/v1/trips/{trip_id}/planning-runs` | List recent runs, bounded by `limit` |
+| `GET /api/v1/planning-runs/{run_id}` | Read lifecycle, failure, and interrupt state |
+| `GET /api/v1/planning-runs/{run_id}/revision` | Read the latest candidate revision |
+| `POST /api/v1/planning-runs/{run_id}/decision` | Resume clarification or final approval |
+
+The direct revision accept/reject endpoints exist for deterministic service and
+test use. Product UI approval should use the run decision endpoint so LangGraph
+checkpoint state and persisted run state advance together.
+
+## Agent v1 acceptance gate
+
+All hard gates must pass at 100%; aggregate model quality is not allowed to hide
+a safety failure.
+
+| Gate | Required result | Evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Structured intent | Compound requests retain all operations and constraints | `test_planning_intents.py` |
+| Grounding and policy | UI context is trip-owned; ambiguity routes to clarification; writes only produce previews | `test_planning_intents.py`, `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Scope enforcement | A candidate cannot change state outside grounded day/activity scope | `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Non-blank run | Missing or whitespace-only request is rejected with 422 | `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Structured output | Extra/unknown data and non-supplied IDs are rejected | `test_openai_planner.py`, `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Prompt-data isolation | Provider instructions declare user and candidate text untrusted | `openai_planner.py`, provider tests |
+| Protected state | Locked, active, and completed activities cannot be changed | `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Evidence integrity | Place, source, route, date, time, and budget constraints are deterministic | `test_planning_runs.py`, `test_planner.py` |
+| Bounded correction | Exactly one correction is permitted; a second failure creates no revision | `test_planning_graph.py` |
+| Goal-aware planning | Explicit goals reach the Planner; hard goals are checked after validation with one bounded repair | `test_planning_goals.py`, `test_openai_planner.py` |
+| Decision Context | Target-day formal route/execution facts are selected with provenance; protected and editable activities are disjoint | `test_decision_context.py`, `test_openai_planner.py` |
+| Human approval | No candidate becomes formal state before acceptance | `test_planning_graph.py`, `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Safe rejection | Rejection leaves the formal plan unchanged | `test_planning_graph.py` |
+| Stale-write protection | A changed trip version prevents acceptance | `test_planning_runs.py` |
+| Recovery | A PostgreSQL checkpoint resumes through a new connection | `test_planning_graph.py` |
+| Provider failures | Missing config, timeout, invalid ID, and correction input have stable behavior | `test_openai_planner.py`, `test_planning_graph.py` |
+| Isolated persistence | Migrations and the complete backend suite pass against the test PostgreSQL service | `compose.test.yaml` |
+
+Before a real-provider milestone is accepted, run one non-private test trip with
+real Planner and map credentials and record: provider/model, request ID if
+available, token usage, elapsed time, route provenance, validation result, and
+the approval/rejection outcome. Never commit the credentials or private payload.
+
+The repeatable intent benchmark is `python -m evals.run_intent_eval`; add
+`--use-model` to require the configured semantic provider. Provider connectivity
+is checked separately with `python -m evals.run_provider_smoke`. A model-enabled
+evaluation exits non-zero when any model call falls back, even if deterministic
+rules still produce the expected route.
+
+## Decision Ledger validation responsibility (2026-09-27)
+
+The Planner still emits `decision_ledger` and the deterministic Ledger validator
+always runs after route enrichment, optional time-only route repair, and the
+candidate itinerary validator. Its result is **not** an itinerary-safety verdict.
+`CandidatePlanSnapshot.provenance_parse_errors` contains only sanitized error
+codes when a provenance field cannot be parsed; place IDs, dates, times, route
+fields and other candidate fields still use strict parsing.
+
+`DECISION_LEDGER_VALIDATION_MODE=shadow` is the production/demo default. In
+`shadow`, an invalid Ledger is persisted as `decision_summary.status=invalid`,
+with `decision_ledger_status` and sanitized `decision_ledger_errors` in the
+revision difference summary and trace, but does not mutate candidate validity.
+`enforced` preserves a blocking Ledger gate for Stage 2.2 evaluation. A valid
+candidate still must pass the independent itinerary/semantics validator and
+hard Goal check before a pending Revision is saved. Acceptance revalidates
+formal-state safety and stale version constraints; neither mode auto-applies a
+Revision. Invalid/unavailable provenance never authorizes the UI to show model
+explanation or activity reasons. Stage 2.2 Decision Ledger MVP remains OPEN.
+
+An unknown/unallowed place is a hard candidate parse/validation failure.
+`unsupported_addition` means an otherwise valid addition lacks Ledger support;
+it is a provenance error only in `shadow`. A removed `must_do` or `can_skip=false`
+activity remains a hard candidate failure regardless of its Ledger reason.
+
+## Change control
+
+### GLM structured-output boundary (2026-09-28)
+
+The configured production model is `glm-5.3-flash` using BigModel Chat
+Completions. GLM JSON Object mode receives the required JSON Schema as output
+guidance; Pydantic and candidate business checks remain authoritative.
+
+For the GLM Planner only, backend-owned UUID references are losslessly encoded
+as request-local `ref_NNNN` tokens throughout structured input and schema enums.
+The same request-local map restores exact identifiers before strict draft
+parsing. Unknown tokens remain invalid; there is no fuzzy/name-based matching,
+substitution of unknown places, or expansion of the allowed-place set. Dates,
+sources, protected state, routes, goals and approval fences remain unchanged.
+This is a provider wire-format adaptation, not a persistence/public API schema
+change; existing checkpoints and revisions require no migration.
+
+The existing single output-repair attempt now receives the previous visible
+invalid output plus validation feedback. Hidden reasoning is never included or
+stored. GLM responses are collected from the streaming API; only visible content,
+the finish marker and usage are retained, never thinking deltas. A stream without
+`stop`, and truncated/filtered Chat Completions, are incomplete even with text.
+Tests: `test_planner_references.py`, `test_model_client.py` and
+the unchanged candidate/approval regression suite.
+
+Future Agent development must read this contract before changing schemas,
+states, nodes, tools, permissions, or failure codes. Any contract change must be
+delivered atomically with:
+
+1. updated Pydantic and database constraints where applicable;
+2. migration compatibility analysis;
+3. unit or integration acceptance tests;
+4. this document updated in the same change;
+5. a passing isolated PostgreSQL backend suite.
+
+Research, additional reviewer loops, cancellation, UI-selection persistence,
+new intents, or automatic completion are contract changes, not incidental
+implementation details.
+
+### Agent Reliability Phase — Entity Resolution & Structured Output Stability (2026-09-28)
+
+All Planner providers now use the same semantic output boundary, implemented in
+`planner_entity_resolution.py`. Existing decisions require `source=existing` and
+`activity_ref=activity_N`; new decisions require `source=candidate` and
+`candidate_ref=candidate_N`. The mutually exclusive strict classes reject
+cross-domain fields. Dynamic schema enums and the server resolver share one
+request-local binding object. Unknown, protected or out-of-scope references are
+not guessed. The resolver restores formal activity/place/source/cost identity
+before route enrichment. Model output contains no database/provider UUID,
+coordinates, route IDs, cost or derived before/after facts.
+
+The internal context builder and persisted CandidatePlanSnapshot retain real
+identifiers for authoritative validation/checkpoints. The provider prompt
+contains each fact partition once, and bounded rejection summaries preserve
+source Revision references while removing redundant compatibility aliases.
+Trip/day/formal-version memory filtering and approval rules remain intact.
+
+Shadow Ledger is no longer required in the model's main JSON Schema. If the
+model supplies a Ledger, it is parsed/quarantined and the existing deterministic
+Ledger validator still runs. An absent/invalid Ledger does not authorize model
+reasons in Web. Stage 2.2 Decision Ledger MVP REMAINS OPEN. Enforced mode still
+rejects missing/unverified Ledger; this phase does not claim its completion.
+
+One structured-output repair remains the maximum. Syntax is parsed by Python
+json without heuristic semantic repair; complete Markdown JSON fences are the
+existing deterministic envelope normalization. Failure details carry
+planner_schema_failure, planner_invalid_reference, planner_malformed_output,
+planner_semantic_constraint_failure, provider_failure or
+candidate_validation_failure. Web masks internal Planner contract errors and
+retries the exact request in an independent PlanningRun.
+
+No persistence schema/migration change is needed: the new short-reference
+contract exists only at the model boundary. Existing saved revisions/checkpoints
+continue to use CandidatePlanSnapshot and transactionally revalidate on accept.
+The dedicated live eval is `python -m evals.agent_reliability.run`; it resets only
+travel-decision-agent-demo, records 10 normal plus 10 Reject/Memory cycles, and
+writes an independent artifact. Its live exit gate is separate from unit tests.
+
+
+## Companion Travel Facts Integration v1 (2026-09-30 working tree)
+
+The existing Companion graph has an additive, read-only FactToolPort for future
+hourly forecasts. InformationNeed contains native FactRequirements; the tool
+composes TravelFactService/freshness/authority and returns native ResolvedFacts.
+Companion cannot call provider HTTP or write formal data. Stable Knowledge and
+forecast server prose remain separate; model generation cannot fill weather gaps.
+Weather and internal location freshness are configured explicitly; only fresh
+resolved provider predictions can be shown. Current/past observations, alerts,
+execution/route/opening/ticket/crowd facts and forecast-driven replanning remain
+outside this Companion slice. Initial product supports Hangzhou/Nanjing city
+representative WGS84 locations with IANA timezone and deterministic hour windows.
+Foundation import is exact 5f9f5e5, not its snapshot parent. Runtime/Router/Brief
+identity and Planning candidate/approval ownership are retained. This extension is deployed in API/worker after final live provider validation
+and regression gates passed. Production smoke7/7 and formal state comparison pass.
+Early network failures are retained as historical attempts; stale/unavailable
+responses still cannot be promoted as verified forecasts. Evidence and limitations:
+docs/agent/TRAVEL_FACTS_INTEGRATION_V1_20260930.md.
+
+
+## Initial candidate editing boundary (2026-10-07 acceptance work)
+
+An explicit day-level adjustment to the current session's pending initial
+candidate may carry a backend-owned `trigger_snapshot.candidate_edit` reference.
+The reference binds the exact revision ID and canonical snapshot hash; it is
+not accepted from public request fields. Only same-trip, same-version, validated,
+pending candidates on an awaiting-confirmation run qualify, while formal
+activities remain empty. A source that changes, is decided, or becomes stale
+fails closed. No arbitrary latest-trip history is selected.
+
+`planning_context.candidate_edit_base` is separate draft-only context. The formal
+`days`, intent entity grounding and Execution authority remain unchanged. The
+Planner can select target-day candidates; places retained on other draft dates
+are excluded from target-day choices. Backend preservation and Validator require
+exact unchanged other-date candidate records. This narrowly permits those
+verified preserved drafts outside the target day; every new or changed record
+outside the target still fails scope validation. Routes, source ownership,
+budget, date and version checks remain mandatory. Approval reloads the referenced
+base before normal validation and formalization. Rejection changes no formal
+state. This is an initial-candidate boundary, not general mutable history.
+
+Compatibility: no public/Pydantic/database schema or migration change; existing
+runs without the optional reference preserve current behavior. Evidence:
+`tests/test_candidate_edit.py` plus graph/planner/run/assistant regression and
+real browser `demo-lifecycle.spec.ts` (full three-day preservation required).
+Production deployment remains pending full acceptance.
+
+
+## Recovery route/budget projection alignment (2026-10-07 acceptance work)
+
+Recovery enrichment now uses the same persisted execution frozen/protected
+activity set as deterministic validation, including the current activity and
+navigation target even while formal status remains planned. All candidates for in-progress or paused trips automatically enforce these
+invariants from persisted execution status, including ordinary Planning requests.
+Recovery/experience callers can also explicitly request the mode. Acceptance
+rechecks the current execution state and retains the same protected activities. Implicit retention uses stable activity references in
+route evidence. Candidate financial summaries include every implicitly retained
+protected activity, including a pending navigation target, without charging
+represented replacements twice. No public/DB schema or migration change.
+Acceptance still revalidates and preserves executed activities; it does not
+relax route completeness. Tests: test_recovery_route_enrichment.py, graph and
+existing navigation/experience recovery regressions. Real failed Recovery and
+incorrect210-versus240 cost evidence are preserved; full final rehearsal pending.
